1
00:05:36,670 --> 00:05:39,049
Tom Lincoln!
it’s a comin'!

2
00:05:40,000 --> 00:05:42,906
-Boy or gal?
-'Taint fur enough along yit fer a tag.

3
00:05:43,406 --> 00:05:46,313
-How's Nancy standin' it?
-She's a-prayin'.

4
00:05:51,277 --> 00:05:52,710
it’s a boy?

5
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:54,418
it’s as long as an eel!

6
00:06:00,322 --> 00:06:04,897
Shucks, he’ never
amount to nothin' nohow!

7
00:06:05,999 --> 00:06:07,473
Is he purty?

8
00:06:08,042 --> 00:06:11,141
Homely as a mud fence!

9
00:06:12,053 --> 00:06:14,206
What do you 'low
to call him, Aunty?

10
00:06:19,130 --> 00:06:20,937
Abraham.

11
00:06:45,910 --> 00:06:50,029
There he is. Ugliest, laziest,
smartest man in New Salem.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/New_Salem,_Illinois}

12
00:06:50,029 --> 00:06:53,034
-Ain't yuh, Abe?
-l don't mind my face, l'm behind it.

13
00:06:53,156 --> 00:06:55,206
it’s the people
in front that get jarred.

14
00:06:56,970 --> 00:07:00,201
Whip any man in shoo
leather l At least, l hope he can.

15
00:07:00,783 --> 00:07:02,910
Offut does the braggin',
l do the runnin'.

16
00:07:03,187 --> 00:07:05,085
You better dust off
them shoes then, stranger,

17
00:07:05,437 --> 00:07:06,651
'cause you ain't here for long.

18
00:07:07,076 --> 00:07:08,898
Well, l was aimin'
to settle here awhile.

19
00:07:09,249 --> 00:07:12,298
-This ain't no peaceable town.
-Well, l'm a peaceable man.

20
00:07:12,606 --> 00:07:14,124
Wait till
Jack Armstrong gets hold o' yuh.

21
00:07:14,437 --> 00:07:16,985
He'll throw yuh out
that door like yuh was a sack o' meal!

22
00:07:17,602 --> 00:07:19,088
-Big fellow?
-No bigger'n you,

23
00:07:19,394 --> 00:07:22,931
but he's champion o' this country
he ain' never been licked.

24
00:07:23,323 --> 00:07:24,961
And when he sees you...

25
00:07:25,077 --> 00:07:27,669
Folks can't even spit
without his sayso.

26
00:07:28,776 --> 00:07:30,566
Chuck me outa that door, you say?

27
00:07:30,836 --> 00:07:32,203
Like a sack o' meal.

28
00:07:34,268 --> 00:07:36,860
Well, that's a pretty long
chuck for a peaceful man.

29
00:07:38,654 --> 00:07:39,911
Whar be he?

30
00:07:40,217 --> 00:07:42,495
Now, Armstrong,
cain't you leave us be?

31
00:07:42,697 --> 00:07:44,823
Yuh just busted up
old man Simpson's place

32
00:07:44,985 --> 00:07:47,425
and scattered
his new place all over the county.

33
00:07:47,501 --> 00:07:49,366
Man, don't you ever rest?

34
00:07:49,599 --> 00:07:54,241
Whare be he?
That yellow-livered baboon from lndianny!

35
00:07:54,977 --> 00:07:58,132
l come from lndianny,
but not that part.

36
00:07:58,485 --> 00:07:59,819
Well, get down!

37
00:08:00,582 --> 00:08:03,327
Let's see what bones
l wants to crack first!

38
00:08:11,452 --> 00:08:12,623
Get a good hold, Jack!

39
00:08:12,787 --> 00:08:14,044
Come on,
an' get yer neck broke!

40
00:08:15,304 --> 00:08:16,475
This is the neatest
wrestle you've ever had.

41
00:08:16,600 --> 00:08:17,663
Go to him, Champ!

42
00:08:17,859 --> 00:08:19,756
-Get 'in, Jack!
-Abraham Lincoln!

43
00:08:20,185 --> 00:08:23,385
-Let's get 'im, Abe come on!
-Get 'in, Jack!

44
00:08:24,572 --> 00:08:29,289
-Get 'im, Jack!
-Atta boy, Abe!

45
00:08:29,605 --> 00:08:31,806
l'll bet yuh ten dollars,
Offut, on the Champeen!

46
00:08:32,122 --> 00:08:33,836
-Watch 'im, boy!
-Git 'im!

47
00:08:34,029 --> 00:08:36,198
-Watch 'im, boy!
-Come on, git 'im!

48
00:08:37,118 --> 00:08:39,363
-Come on, git 'im!
-Git 'im!

49
00:08:41,084 --> 00:08:42,722
Watch 'im, boy!

50
00:08:44,631 --> 00:08:46,800
Come on, git 'im!

51
00:09:02,480 --> 00:09:03,694
Now, come on, all o' you.

52
00:09:03,853 --> 00:09:05,370
l'm the big tuck of thisn lick!

53
00:09:05,493 --> 00:09:06,859
Yuh are, eh?

54
00:09:08,886 --> 00:09:10,057
Hold on, boys!

55
00:09:10,640 --> 00:09:11,898
He throwed me fair.

56
00:09:13,463 --> 00:09:14,829
What's your name?

57
00:09:15,637 --> 00:09:18,261
Lincoln,...
Abraham Lincoln.

58
00:09:19,107 --> 00:09:20,972
Well, we're goin' to make yuh one of us.

59
00:09:21,585 --> 00:09:23,333
Come on, boys, l'll set 'em up!

60
00:09:23,608 --> 00:09:24,897
it’s about time you set 'em up.

61
00:09:29,900 --> 00:09:31,993
Let's have some o'
that rattlesnake bite.

62
00:09:32,760 --> 00:09:34,203
He said he was a peaceful man.

63
00:09:34,629 --> 00:09:36,342
He meant peaceful like a wildcat!

64
00:09:36,459 --> 00:09:37,825
Yeh, that's what he thought.

65
00:09:38,938 --> 00:09:40,500
Looks like good corn liquor.

66
00:09:40,769 --> 00:09:42,286
That is corn liquor.

67
00:09:44,584 --> 00:09:46,101
Come on, Abe. Where's yours?

68
00:09:46,604 --> 00:09:48,242
No, l don't care much for it.

69
00:09:48,358 --> 00:09:50,147
Come on, have a drink, Abe.

70
00:09:50,989 --> 00:09:52,888
Well, l'll drink
if you'll take it like l do.

71
00:09:53,279 --> 00:09:54,374
How's that?

72
00:09:55,071 --> 00:09:57,044
Lift up the barrel,
and drink it outa the bung.

73
00:09:59,037 --> 00:10:00,207
Well, l'll show you boys.

74
00:10:02,049 --> 00:10:05,705
Maybe can, maybe can't.
Try it, anyway.

75
00:10:21,690 --> 00:10:23,057
Whew!

76
00:10:24,513 --> 00:10:26,226
He's the strongest man in Salem!

77
00:10:26,533 --> 00:10:28,280
l told you he was the strongest man.

78
00:10:28,478 --> 00:10:31,027
l've seen him lift
500 pounds in each hand.

79
00:10:33,895 --> 00:10:35,836
-Ain't yuh drinkin?
-No.

80
00:10:36,907 --> 00:10:38,393
-Teetotal?
-No.

81
00:10:39,005 --> 00:10:40,219
Reformer?

82
00:10:40,682 --> 00:10:42,960
No, l don't
regulate nobody's drinkin'

83
00:10:43,734 --> 00:10:44,872
just my own.

84
00:10:46,060 --> 00:10:48,501
l've seen him lift a thousand...

85
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:54,812
-ln this sense,-
-the term ''law''-

86
00:10:55,691 --> 00:11:00,617
-includes any edict,-
-decree, order,-

87
00:11:01,338 --> 00:11:06,372
-ordinance,-
-statute, resolution,-

88
00:11:07,062 --> 00:11:09,594
-rule, etcetera.-

89
00:11:10,700 --> 00:11:14,063
¡Uff!, etcetera...

90
00:11:20,213 --> 00:11:23,652
Well, my old Daddy
taught me how to work,

91
00:11:23,852 --> 00:11:25,555
but he never
taught me how to like it.

92
00:11:28,893 --> 00:11:31,470
l reckon l better
keep on with the lesson.

93
00:11:32,645 --> 00:11:34,984
l'd rather keep on
with something else.

94
00:11:35,564 --> 00:11:39,724
You made a bad bargain
making me the professor.

95
00:11:40,112 --> 00:11:42,149
Well he told me about that too.

96
00:11:42,954 --> 00:11:44,992
He said:
''When you make a bad bargain,

97
00:11:46,101 --> 00:11:47,578
hug it all the tighter.''

98
00:11:49,436 --> 00:11:52,163
But he didn't mean
this kind of a bargain.

99
00:11:53,681 --> 00:11:57,238
Well, don't you like it,
Ann, when l hold you tight?

100
00:12:00,428 --> 00:12:03,004
l guess every girl sorta likes that...

101
00:12:05,469 --> 00:12:09,478
Now, Abe, what is law?

102
00:12:10,699 --> 00:12:16,877
Well, professor, law is a rule
of human conduct governing

103
00:12:17,105 --> 00:12:18,312
Whoa!

104
00:12:20,099 --> 00:12:24,983
Abe, l'ain't payin' you no forty
cents a day to spark a pretty gal!

105
00:12:25,444 --> 00:12:27,869
Well, Uncle Jimmie,
l don't charge nothing extra for it

106
00:12:28,021 --> 00:12:29,184
just throw it in.

107
00:12:32,833 --> 00:12:35,670
l'm expectin'
a sight of rails cut of you.

108
00:12:36,472 --> 00:12:38,251
He's the best
rail-splitter in the country.

109
00:12:38,860 --> 00:12:41,166
He'll be more than a rail-splitter.

110
00:12:42,461 --> 00:12:44,046
How old are you, Uncle Jimmie?

111
00:12:45,228 --> 00:12:46,554
Oh nearin' forty.

112
00:12:47,312 --> 00:12:50,266
l'll get you out forty
more rails than you expected.

113
00:12:53,073 --> 00:12:56,557
Giddap! Maybe you'd better
make it nearin' seventy!

114
00:13:07,742 --> 00:13:11,751
Now, Abe,
your professor needs a seat

115
00:13:11,911 --> 00:13:14,562
where's there's more law
and less temptation.

116
00:13:20,401 --> 00:13:22,254
Oh, Abe! Ohhh!

117
00:13:24,116 --> 00:13:26,045
Ohhhh!

118
00:13:27,034 --> 00:13:28,921
-Are you all right, Anne?
-l think so.

119
00:13:29,081 --> 00:13:31,884
-Are you hurt?
-My legs are still on me.

120
00:13:34,880 --> 00:13:36,508
Scared me worse than it did you.

121
00:13:36,927 --> 00:13:38,285
Did it, Abe?

122
00:13:40,186 --> 00:13:45,792
You know, Anne, if anything happened
to you, l don't think l could live.

123
00:13:46,857 --> 00:13:50,446
Funny, Abe, l feel the same about you.

124
00:13:52,239 --> 00:13:53,791
You taught me how to love.

125
00:13:55,195 --> 00:13:57,383
Have l taught you to like it?

126
00:14:06,225 --> 00:14:13,038
-ln the gloaming, oh, my darling,-

127
00:14:13,464 --> 00:14:21,193
-When the lights are dim and low,-

128
00:14:22,068 --> 00:14:26,607
-And the quiet shadows falling.-

129
00:14:26,806 --> 00:14:35,409
-Softly come and softly go.-

130
00:14:36,964 --> 00:14:38,624
it’s awful nice this time of day.

131
00:14:39,389 --> 00:14:40,747
Yes, Abe.

132
00:14:41,171 --> 00:14:43,283
Can l tell you
a sort of a story, Anne?

133
00:14:43,634 --> 00:14:45,747
Why of course, Abe.

134
00:14:45,947 --> 00:14:48,749
Well, there was
a town in lllinois called...

135
00:14:49,737 --> 00:14:50,900
called...

136
00:14:50,987 --> 00:14:52,992
-New Salem?
-Yeh, that's it.

137
00:14:53,678 --> 00:14:57,009
And in that town lived
the prettiest girl in the world.

138
00:14:57,468 --> 00:14:58,826
What was her name?

139
00:14:59,098 --> 00:15:00,348
Anne Rutledge.

140
00:15:00,955 --> 00:15:02,237
Oh, Abe...

141
00:15:04,784 --> 00:15:07,435
What l'd like
to find out about that girl is,

142
00:15:08,119 --> 00:15:11,946
she ever take a little time off
to think about gettin' married?

143
00:15:12,554 --> 00:15:14,138
Well, maybe.

144
00:15:14,525 --> 00:15:17,360
'Cause there's an Abe Lincoln
hanging around that's a pretty good catch.

145
00:15:17,936 --> 00:15:19,218
What's he like?

146
00:15:19,944 --> 00:15:24,332
Oh, he's a big merchant,
owner of three stores all bankrupt.

147
00:15:24,834 --> 00:15:27,410
Well, is he handsome?

148
00:15:29,534 --> 00:15:32,595
His pa said that Abe
had been cut out with an axe.

149
00:15:32,830 --> 00:15:34,760
Politician too, l hear.

150
00:15:35,598 --> 00:15:38,249
Yes, he's got less property
and owes more debts

151
00:15:38,441 --> 00:15:40,402
than anybody
ever running for the legislature.

152
00:15:41,056 --> 00:15:44,160
Oh, Abe, you'll do all
right when you get started.

153
00:15:48,523 --> 00:15:52,532
There's something l'd like to start
right now if l thought l could finish it.

154
00:15:54,133 --> 00:15:55,458
You know, Anne, l...

155
00:15:56,065 --> 00:15:58,027
l've always done a lot of dreamin'

156
00:15:59,211 --> 00:16:01,366
and lately it seems when l dream,

157
00:16:02,622 --> 00:16:04,702
your face gets mixed up in it.

158
00:16:06,716 --> 00:16:11,900
Does it really, Abe?
Tell me about the mixin'.

159
00:16:12,932 --> 00:16:18,613
l feel as though l'm going to be
seeing your face till the day l die.

160
00:16:22,407 --> 00:16:26,568
'Course l know that would be pretty hard
for you to have to look at my face that long.

161
00:16:27,486 --> 00:16:29,523
Everybody to their own opinion

162
00:16:31,883 --> 00:16:33,208
Meaning?

163
00:16:35,484 --> 00:16:36,917
l think...

164
00:16:37,340 --> 00:16:42,902
it's the dearest, kindest,
most beautiful face in the whole world!

165
00:16:43,026 --> 00:16:47,112
Oh, Anne...
Anne...

166
00:16:51,440 --> 00:16:54,275
l know
that's just flattery but l love it.

167
00:16:56,103 --> 00:16:58,937
You know
l feel like little Jimmie Watkins,

168
00:16:59,135 --> 00:17:01,171
he got a hunk of gingerbread
the other day and says:

169
00:17:01,408 --> 00:17:04,363
''l guess there's nobody
likes ginger bread like l does and

170
00:17:06,412 --> 00:17:07,694
gets so little of it''.

171
00:17:08,458 --> 00:17:09,891
Oh, Abe!

172
00:17:12,324 --> 00:17:13,606
Anne, will you...

173
00:17:14,826 --> 00:17:16,076
will you marry me?

174
00:17:17,517 --> 00:17:18,918
l mean, of course when l...

175
00:17:19,147 --> 00:17:21,723
when l...
get out of debt and can support you?

176
00:17:22,785 --> 00:17:27,862
Well, you know, Abe,
l've intended to for a long while

177
00:17:29,456 --> 00:17:33,013
that is, of course, if you asked me.

178
00:17:34,194 --> 00:17:37,708
You... you mean?

179
00:17:38,098 --> 00:17:42,830
Yes, Abe, you've got your gingerbread.

180
00:17:44,845 --> 00:17:46,397
Oh, Anne!

181
00:17:51,174 --> 00:18:01,814
-Will you think of me and love me-

182
00:18:02,620 --> 00:18:12,203
-As you did once long ago?-

183
00:18:18,770 --> 00:18:19,974
Pretty bad.

184
00:18:22,361 --> 00:18:24,018
She's been asking for you, Abe.

185
00:18:24,328 --> 00:18:25,682
l came as soon as l could.

186
00:18:27,352 --> 00:18:30,029
l had the fever pretty bad myself.

187
00:18:36,500 --> 00:18:39,295
l've got to tell you the truth, Abe,

188
00:18:39,977 --> 00:18:41,601
it’s hopeless...

189
00:18:43,683 --> 00:18:45,081
perhaps by tomorrow...

190
00:18:46,405 --> 00:18:48,061
no longer.

191
00:19:13,434 --> 00:19:17,401
l'm so glad you came, Abe.

192
00:19:19,483 --> 00:19:21,256
Are you all right now?

193
00:19:21,410 --> 00:19:24,959
Now, don't bother about me, dear.
l'm all right now.

194
00:19:27,043 --> 00:19:32,742
l know the truth, dear.
it’s good-bye.

195
00:19:33,281 --> 00:19:37,023
No, no, Anne dear,
you're not going to leave me!

196
00:19:37,703 --> 00:19:38,865
l won't let you!

197
00:19:39,594 --> 00:19:43,067
We must be brave, dear...

198
00:19:46,814 --> 00:19:50,782
Don't take me away!
Don't take me away!

199
00:19:52,409 --> 00:19:57,431
it’s so dark and lonesome going!

200
00:19:57,664 --> 00:20:00,191
Anne, you mustn't let go!

201
00:20:02,578 --> 00:20:07,642
lf they'd sing,
l wouldn't be so afraid.

202
00:20:10,366 --> 00:20:16,215
-ln the sweet by and by,-

203
00:20:16,680 --> 00:20:21,926
-We shall meet on that beautiful shore.-

204
00:20:22,198 --> 00:20:26,017
We will meet there, dear.

205
00:20:26,924 --> 00:20:28,773
Oh... l love you so!

206
00:20:30,288 --> 00:20:33,158
l love you so!

207
00:21:09,378 --> 00:21:10,581
Feeling any better, Bowling?

208
00:21:11,230 --> 00:21:12,583
No, not much.

209
00:21:13,082 --> 00:21:15,986
l'll tell you, Doctor,
he's just like a sick child...

210
00:21:16,258 --> 00:21:19,010
He was lost for five days
before we found him.

211
00:21:19,131 --> 00:21:20,454
Yeh, l know.

212
00:21:20,454 --> 00:21:25,206
We took his pocket-knife away from him,
we were so afeard something might happen.

213
00:21:26,956 --> 00:21:29,827
Good gracious,
Abe, you're looking better!

214
00:21:41,094 --> 00:21:42,675
No use trying to talk to him,

215
00:21:43,137 --> 00:21:44,760
he just cain't answer.

216
00:22:04,722 --> 00:22:07,668
lf we could only think of something
that would just bring him back.

217
00:22:10,468 --> 00:22:12,392
l guess time's the only thing.

218
00:22:15,269 --> 00:22:19,946
Why should the spirit of mortal be proud,

219
00:22:21,129 --> 00:22:25,204
like a swift-fleeting meteor,
fast-flying cloud?

220
00:22:26,686 --> 00:22:30,265
Flash of the lightning,
break of the wave,

221
00:22:32,810 --> 00:22:35,756
He passes from life
to his rest in the grave.

222
00:25:19,940 --> 00:25:21,758
My goodness, Mary Todd, just think!

223
00:25:22,033 --> 00:25:25,107
He'll be here in a few minutes
the catch of America!

224
00:25:25,344 --> 00:25:27,358
Stephen A. Douglas!{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stephen_A._Douglas}

225
00:25:28,350 --> 00:25:30,212
Just think of being his wife!

226
00:25:30,520 --> 00:25:31,927
Don't be in such a hurry, sister.

227
00:25:32,043 --> 00:25:34,705
l'm not even engaged
much less married.

228
00:25:35,505 --> 00:25:36,988
But if he should propose?

229
00:25:37,714 --> 00:25:40,755
How do l know he's going further
than anyone else in Springfield?

230
00:25:41,671 --> 00:25:44,896
When l pick a husband, sister,
l'll pick a man!

231
00:25:45,286 --> 00:25:46,767
But l don't know
what you're talking about.

232
00:25:47,190 --> 00:25:51,033
A lot of people seem to think
a man named Abraham Lincoln

233
00:25:51,451 --> 00:25:53,270
is going even further than Mr. Douglas.

234
00:25:53,430 --> 00:25:55,898
Why, Mary Todd, have you gone crazy?

235
00:25:56,627 --> 00:26:00,156
You compare an unknown cornfield lawyer

236
00:26:00,319 --> 00:26:03,771
with a brilliant cultured gentleman
like Stephen A. Douglas!

237
00:26:03,897 --> 00:26:05,638
Why, if you just saw
the two of them together.

238
00:26:05,837 --> 00:26:07,093
Oh, he's here!

239
00:26:07,322 --> 00:26:10,017
Mr. Douglas is down in the parlor
and he's asking for Mary!

240
00:26:10,823 --> 00:26:12,869
Now, Mary, you must be
very careful, and remember

241
00:26:13,106 --> 00:26:14,773
he doesn't like to have girls too bold.

242
00:26:14,971 --> 00:26:17,969
Don't get so excited,
sister, and don't hurry me.

243
00:26:18,701 --> 00:26:20,335
l'll take care of myself.

244
00:26:47,739 --> 00:26:50,628
You glide through the dance
like grace itself Miss Todd.

245
00:26:50,935 --> 00:26:53,446
Always the politician, Mr. Douglas!

246
00:26:56,986 --> 00:27:00,709
Who wouldn't be a politician
with so fair a constituent to win?

247
00:27:42,427 --> 00:27:45,392
-Exquisite!
-The fan, Mr. Douglas?

248
00:27:45,509 --> 00:27:47,706
No, the fair owner herself.

249
00:27:52,854 --> 00:27:54,294
-May l look?
-Sr. Douglas...

250
00:27:55,251 --> 00:27:57,265
l wonder if you'd do me a great favor?

251
00:27:57,993 --> 00:27:59,129
Yes.

252
00:27:59,858 --> 00:28:02,022
Then, pray present me
to this young lady.

253
00:28:03,586 --> 00:28:05,026
-Miss Todd?
-Yes?

254
00:28:05,603 --> 00:28:09,826
May l present one
of the leading lawyers of Springfield

255
00:28:10,000 --> 00:28:11,441
Mr. Abraham Lincoln?

256
00:28:12,742 --> 00:28:13,998
Mr. Lincoln!

257
00:28:19,218 --> 00:28:22,109
Miss Todd l wonder
if you'd honor me with the next dance?

258
00:28:23,065 --> 00:28:25,837
Why,... why...

259
00:28:28,283 --> 00:28:29,876
l'd be delighted.

260
00:28:34,110 --> 00:28:36,234
Miss Todd, you thought my face was funny,

261
00:28:36,472 --> 00:28:37,913
and the way l dressed even funnier,

262
00:28:38,149 --> 00:28:39,362
but the joke's on you.

263
00:28:40,777 --> 00:28:42,108
Why, l don't understand.

264
00:28:43,176 --> 00:28:44,659
Wait till you dance with me.

265
00:29:13,495 --> 00:29:15,206
Have you got the license, Mr. Lincoln?

266
00:29:25,493 --> 00:29:26,663
Well, she got you.

267
00:29:27,130 --> 00:29:29,481
l knew she would
when she started out the first time for you.

268
00:29:29,760 --> 00:29:30,929
Now, Billie, don't bother me.

269
00:29:31,054 --> 00:29:32,992
l'm going to be married
and l'm scared to death.

270
00:29:34,101 --> 00:29:35,237
Oh, don't be alarmed.

271
00:29:35,434 --> 00:29:37,374
There's many a bite
that's worse than a bride's.

272
00:29:38,063 --> 00:29:41,138
But, Billie,
that woman scares me.

273
00:29:44,575 --> 00:29:48,227
She's even got the ridiculous idea
that l could get to be president.

274
00:29:48,385 --> 00:29:50,247
Oh, don't take that seriously.

275
00:29:50,518 --> 00:29:53,258
Every spunky girl thinks
that her husband ought to be president.

276
00:29:53,717 --> 00:29:55,579
l know, Billie,
but it's a pity to fool her.

277
00:29:55,965 --> 00:29:57,406
And she's a fine woman,

278
00:29:57,679 --> 00:29:59,726
smart as pepper and pretty too.

279
00:29:59,964 --> 00:30:01,600
She'll be a great help to you, Mr. Lincoln,

280
00:30:01,830 --> 00:30:03,726
but you've got to keep
climbing with her.

281
00:30:04,344 --> 00:30:06,358
Yes... yes, l know.

282
00:30:07,353 --> 00:30:09,368
l've got the best supper
you've ever tasted

283
00:30:09,600 --> 00:30:12,222
and the cake...

284
00:30:12,685 --> 00:30:14,127
wait till you see that cake!

285
00:30:14,323 --> 00:30:16,110
What can have happened
to Mr. Lincoln? it’s long after the...

286
00:30:16,228 --> 00:30:18,199
He would be late at his own wedding.

287
00:30:19,162 --> 00:30:20,331
Now, never mind, Mary,

288
00:30:20,531 --> 00:30:22,850
if he doesn't come soon,
l'll send John after him.

289
00:30:22,931 --> 00:30:24,524
But he's hours late already!

290
00:30:24,798 --> 00:30:25,967
Think of that!

291
00:30:26,092 --> 00:30:28,444
Sister, calm yourself.

292
00:30:30,549 --> 00:30:32,370
For heaven's sakes,
you've got to hurry!

293
00:30:34,053 --> 00:30:37,781
Billie, you... you go ahead.
You go on over and l'll come later.

294
00:30:38,891 --> 00:30:41,968
l doubt if there's a word in the
dictionary that could tell how l feel!

295
00:30:42,357 --> 00:30:46,127
Billie, what does a man do
if his head's all right

296
00:30:46,319 --> 00:30:47,727
but his legs are cowardly?

297
00:30:47,842 --> 00:30:49,206
My cure is to get drunk.

298
00:30:50,127 --> 00:30:52,868
My legs are too frightened
to pay any attention to liquor.

299
00:30:53,403 --> 00:30:54,995
l'll go ahead and
tell them that you're coming.

300
00:31:07,687 --> 00:31:08,823
Anne!

301
00:31:13,819 --> 00:31:14,913
Anne!

302
00:31:19,000 --> 00:31:20,169
Anne!

303
00:31:25,481 --> 00:31:27,949
We looked, everywhere,
tort we can't find him.

304
00:31:28,145 --> 00:31:30,310
No, can't find him...
not at the office.

305
00:31:30,580 --> 00:31:31,912
What on earth happened to him?

306
00:31:32,103 --> 00:31:33,921
He was in a terrible
state when l left him.

307
00:31:34,158 --> 00:31:37,231
He was so frightened and upset.
l imagine he just ran away.

308
00:31:37,469 --> 00:31:41,572
Ran away... from me!
On our weddin' day!

309
00:31:41,769 --> 00:31:42,905
Now sistern dear...

310
00:31:43,064 --> 00:31:44,199
Don't 'sister' me!

311
00:31:44,358 --> 00:31:49,185
Can you imagine! That's what a Todd gets
for engagin' herself to a country baboon!

312
00:31:49,420 --> 00:31:51,811
-Listen, dear, you mustn't...
-You may as well all go home!

313
00:31:52,426 --> 00:31:54,363
You hear me?
You may as well all go home!

314
00:31:54,671 --> 00:31:55,839
Not goin' to be any weddin' here.

315
00:31:55,964 --> 00:31:57,523
You can all go home
as far as l'm concerned!

316
00:31:58,820 --> 00:32:00,606
But this is so distressing!

317
00:32:05,328 --> 00:32:07,264
You certainly are a matchmaker

318
00:32:07,762 --> 00:32:10,383
to reconcile those two after
what happened two years ago.

319
00:32:10,465 --> 00:32:12,099
We all like Mr. Lincoln,

320
00:32:12,330 --> 00:32:14,798
and Mary is just
the girl to push him along.

321
00:32:16,212 --> 00:32:18,527
Oh, Mary, how sweet you look!

322
00:32:18,724 --> 00:32:19,860
What a lovely dress!

323
00:32:20,055 --> 00:32:22,177
Yes, and so becoming to her too.

324
00:32:22,682 --> 00:32:24,316
Maybe that's Mr. Lincoln now.

325
00:32:24,965 --> 00:32:28,266
Julia it all seems so strange
having things end this way.

326
00:32:28,618 --> 00:32:31,660
it’s just as it should end. You and
Mr. Lincoln will make a great man.

327
00:32:33,490 --> 00:32:35,383
-Good morning, Mr. Lincoln.

328
00:32:35,925 --> 00:32:37,061
Good morning.

329
00:32:40,074 --> 00:32:42,585
Come, my dear
we'll leave the happy pair alone.

330
00:32:42,889 --> 00:32:43,982
Good morning, Mary.

331
00:32:47,380 --> 00:32:50,650
l,... l...

332
00:32:54,307 --> 00:32:57,532
Mary, you don't have
to bother about me any more.

333
00:32:57,884 --> 00:32:59,822
l think l've settled down at last.

334
00:33:00,854 --> 00:33:03,775
l hope l can make
your future all you desire.

335
00:33:04,163 --> 00:33:06,252
We'll say no more about it, Mr. Lincoln.

336
00:33:07,285 --> 00:33:10,359
l really think, after all, you need me.

337
00:33:13,564 --> 00:33:16,075
You'll need a lot of patience
to put up with me, Mary,

338
00:33:17,103 --> 00:33:20,632
but if anyone can do it,
l'm sure you're the one.

339
00:33:24,677 --> 00:33:25,845
Oh, Mary!

340
00:33:59,652 --> 00:34:01,773
Douglas is a prize speaker,

341
00:34:01,973 --> 00:34:05,470
but just wait till old Abe
gets a hold of him on slavery!

342
00:34:05,778 --> 00:34:06,990
You're crazy!

343
00:34:07,110 --> 00:34:10,834
Lincoln has no more chance of beating
Douglas for Senate than l have.

344
00:34:11,639 --> 00:34:13,576
He only went to school for three months.

345
00:34:30,000 --> 00:34:34,098
There must be no issue of slavery.

346
00:34:37,754 --> 00:34:40,448
We must face the facts.

347
00:34:45,889 --> 00:34:51,589
l'll run Mr. Lincoln out of this campaign.

348
00:34:56,228 --> 00:34:58,046
l will not throw mud.

349
00:35:05,771 --> 00:35:13,102
Let each State mind its own business
and this Republic can exist forever...

350
00:35:13,830 --> 00:35:18,155
divided into free and slave states.

351
00:35:29,301 --> 00:35:34,696
We will not allow
the extension of slavery to any State.

352
00:35:37,397 --> 00:35:41,116
We will not allow
the secession of any State.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Secession_in_the_United_States}

353
00:35:43,708 --> 00:35:50,963
Above all and before all,
the Union must be preserved.

354
00:35:56,518 --> 00:36:00,962
A house divided
against itself must fall!

355
00:36:10,051 --> 00:36:13,467
--Douglas elected!-
--Hurrah for Douglas!-

356
00:36:13,775 --> 00:36:17,268
--Douglas elected!-
--Hurrah for Douglas!-

357
00:36:17,957 --> 00:36:24,790
--Douglas elected!-
--Hurrah for Douglas!-

358
00:36:38,447 --> 00:36:39,539
Mary...

359
00:36:47,758 --> 00:36:49,090
l'm home, Mary.

360
00:36:55,361 --> 00:36:57,482
You must be tired, Mr. Lincoln.

361
00:36:59,848 --> 00:37:00,983
Sit down.

362
00:37:11,860 --> 00:37:16,033
You just ait right here.
l'll get your supper for you in a minute.

363
00:37:30,373 --> 00:37:33,066
Billy, l feel like
the little boy who stubbed his toe.

364
00:37:33,223 --> 00:37:35,882
lt hurt too bad to laugh,
and he was too big to cry.

365
00:37:38,203 --> 00:37:41,124
l'm fifty years old, Billy,
and a failure in everything.

366
00:37:42,005 --> 00:37:44,544
lf l died today,
nobody'd ever know l'd lived.

367
00:37:46,718 --> 00:37:47,852
Come in.

368
00:37:52,306 --> 00:37:54,922
Mr. Lincoln,
l want you to meet Mr. Pell

369
00:37:55,348 --> 00:37:58,385
one of the most important
men in Eastern politics.

370
00:37:59,110 --> 00:38:01,727
-l'm pleased to meet you, Mr. Pell.
-l'm honored, indeed, Mr. Lincoln.

371
00:38:01,886 --> 00:38:04,350
-Meet my partner, Mr. Herndon.
-How do you do, Mr. Herndon?

372
00:38:04,470 --> 00:38:05,562
Happy to meet you, sir.

373
00:38:05,876 --> 00:38:07,811
-Won't you sit down, gentlemen?
-Thank you.

374
00:38:08,501 --> 00:38:12,749
Mr. Lincoln, your campaign against
Douglas has made you a national figure.

375
00:38:13,593 --> 00:38:17,355
l'm here to ask you if you will consider
being the Republican party's candidate

376
00:38:17,736 --> 00:38:19,218
for the presidency.

377
00:38:26,062 --> 00:38:28,679
Did you say a failure in everything?

378
00:38:33,019 --> 00:38:34,759
-A telegram for you, Mr. Fell.
-Oh thank you.

379
00:38:42,065 --> 00:38:44,531
Mr. Lincoln, you know
ever since John Brown's raid,

380
00:38:45,296 --> 00:38:48,410
the South has been infuriated,
the East on the verge of revolt,

381
00:38:48,527 --> 00:38:50,766
and now New York
threatens to quit the Union.

382
00:38:50,923 --> 00:38:52,662
No, no New York mustn't do that.

383
00:38:53,127 --> 00:38:56,619
We must keep the front door on the hinge.
There can be no secession.

384
00:38:58,866 --> 00:39:01,105
The Union must be preserved.

385
00:39:01,528 --> 00:39:06,469
The crisis is at hand, Mr. Lincoln,
and we believe you are the man.

386
00:39:11,526 --> 00:39:15,590
Gentlemen,
l feel deeply grateful.

387
00:39:17,569 --> 00:39:20,489
Paw, Maw says if you don't come on home,
you won't get no supper.

388
00:39:21,142 --> 00:39:26,041
''l have another crisis; the soup and
the country are boiling over together!''

389
00:39:26,387 --> 00:39:27,869
Maw's boiling too!

390
00:39:31,482 --> 00:39:33,569
lt needs deep consideration.

391
00:39:34,485 --> 00:39:38,010
Well, Mr. Lincoln,
mayn't we meet you at our hotel later?

392
00:39:40,606 --> 00:39:42,615
-l'll be there within the hour?
-Thank you.

393
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:45,767
Come on, Paw, we're hungry.

394
00:40:04,317 --> 00:40:07,239
They've started it.
This is gonna mean war!

395
00:40:07,362 --> 00:40:09,029
This darkio saw it himself.

396
00:40:09,454 --> 00:40:13,525
Brown and a gang of Abolitionists
have captured the armory at Harper's Ferry!

397
00:40:14,022 --> 00:40:16,836
They're arming the slaves
to rise up and murder us all.

398
00:40:17,065 --> 00:40:19,501
They gived us all rifles.

399
00:40:19,654 --> 00:40:20,867
And what'd you do with yours?

400
00:40:21,063 --> 00:40:26,766
What'd l do? l trowed it down,
and l say, 'Feet, you travel'.

401
00:40:27,075 --> 00:40:29,813
-They can't invade Virginia.
-No!

402
00:40:30,005 --> 00:40:32,442
Boys, go home and git your guns.

403
00:40:32,632 --> 00:40:33,768
What's all this talk about guns?

404
00:40:33,926 --> 00:40:35,483
This thing has gone far enough.

405
00:40:35,676 --> 00:40:38,296
We'll be murdered
in our beds by our own slaves.

406
00:40:38,454 --> 00:40:41,572
Pardon me, ladies,
while l find out about this desecration.

407
00:40:41,690 --> 00:40:43,963
-John Brown, eh?
-Abolitionists!

408
00:40:44,163 --> 00:40:45,255
-it’s an outrage!

409
00:40:45,381 --> 00:40:47,199
Outrage isn't the word.

410
00:40:48,235 --> 00:40:54,319
l'll shoot on sight every Abolitionist
who dares defile the soil of old Virginia.

411
00:40:54,972 --> 00:40:57,667
-Who's he?
-That's the actor, John Wilkes Booth.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/John_Wilkes_Booth}

412
00:40:57,978 --> 00:41:00,023
He can't act
but the women don't know it.

413
00:41:00,337 --> 00:41:04,440
All right, men, get your guns
and we'll meet at the square!

414
00:41:04,829 --> 00:41:08,130
At the square!

415
00:41:40,069 --> 00:41:41,931
Can't say much for her disposition!

416
00:41:42,162 --> 00:41:43,450
Hush! She may hear you.

417
00:41:43,608 --> 00:41:45,882
Soldiers, indeed!
They can't even carry trunks.

418
00:41:46,120 --> 00:41:48,242
Here you no, the stupid-looking one

419
00:41:48,442 --> 00:41:51,257
put that over there...
and you... idiot

420
00:41:51,411 --> 00:41:53,727
don't look at me like
a duck in a thunderstorm!

421
00:41:55,103 --> 00:41:57,072
Hurry... hurry!
Will you put them in there?

422
00:41:57,575 --> 00:41:59,513
l never saw such a lot of incompetents.

423
00:41:59,897 --> 00:42:02,289
And as for you with the whiskers,
l told you to put it in there.

424
00:42:04,084 --> 00:42:05,566
Oh, is it going to take forever?

425
00:42:09,069 --> 00:42:10,887
Thank heavens, that's the last!

426
00:42:11,886 --> 00:42:13,098
imbeciles!

427
00:42:13,940 --> 00:42:15,272
Well, Mary, we're here.

428
00:42:15,577 --> 00:42:19,105
Thanks to me! lf it wasn't for my advice,
you'd be out in Oregon, chopping trees.

429
00:42:19,648 --> 00:42:21,618
Yes, you're always right, Mary.

430
00:42:27,908 --> 00:42:29,995
l've found out one thing, Lincoln.

431
00:42:30,953 --> 00:42:33,225
Servants here are no better
than they are in Springfield.

432
00:42:35,823 --> 00:42:38,291
This place
hasn't been cleaned in a year.

433
00:42:41,038 --> 00:42:43,083
Why, Mr. President!

434
00:42:46,175 --> 00:42:47,344
Huh?

435
00:42:53,368 --> 00:42:57,211
Then we agree that the situation
of our country is ominous.

436
00:42:57,364 --> 00:42:59,182
-Most alarming.
-Certainly.

437
00:42:59,343 --> 00:43:02,796
We agree that we must yield
to the demands of the South

438
00:43:03,795 --> 00:43:06,306
and evacuate Fort Sumter.{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Battle_of_Fort_Sumter}

439
00:43:07,106 --> 00:43:08,924
-Absolutely.
-it’s the only solution.

440
00:43:09,162 --> 00:43:10,416
-That must be done.

441
00:43:10,493 --> 00:43:14,671
We agree that our President
must be firmly guided by us.

442
00:43:16,240 --> 00:43:21,523
That we must make every effort
to control his inexperienced judgment.

443
00:43:22,292 --> 00:43:25,408
-We certainly must.
-Yes.

444
00:43:30,930 --> 00:43:32,143
Gentlemen...

445
00:43:32,263 --> 00:43:34,123
-Mr. President.
-Good morning, Mr. President.

446
00:43:34,280 --> 00:43:35,492
Thank you, Hay.

447
00:44:12,440 --> 00:44:13,691
Thank you, gentlemen...

448
00:44:14,958 --> 00:44:22,877
l will shoulder all responsibilities.
The relief shall go to Fort Sumter.

449
00:44:24,436 --> 00:44:26,027
That means war.

450
00:44:27,065 --> 00:44:30,982
Mr. Seward, l am a man of peace,{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/William_H._Seward}

451
00:44:32,470 --> 00:44:35,355
but the Union shall be preserved.

452
00:45:02,700 --> 00:45:04,574
Seventy-five thousand...

453
00:45:06,330 --> 00:45:09,118
lt might be difficult
to get that many volunteers.

454
00:48:12,980 --> 00:48:17,928
Mr. President, the people demand a victory,
and we've got to take Richmond.

455
00:48:26,166 --> 00:48:29,547
The country is discouraged.
We must do something.

456
00:48:30,142 --> 00:48:32,572
That reminds me of a story...

457
00:48:33,055 --> 00:48:35,732
about a man building
a boat to cross a river.

458
00:48:36,402 --> 00:48:39,502
He got impatient
building the boat and started swimming.

459
00:48:41,124 --> 00:48:42,614
Well, sir, what happened?

460
00:48:43,723 --> 00:48:44,898
He drowned.

461
00:48:45,770 --> 00:48:49,498
We've got to be careful
not to drown this country.

462
00:48:59,942 --> 00:49:01,632
Ready here!
Ready here!

463
00:49:02,973 --> 00:49:04,226
-Quartermaster General.
-Yes, sir.

464
00:49:04,430 --> 00:49:05,807
Ready here!
Ready here!

465
00:49:08,721 --> 00:49:10,836
-Ready here!
-Sergeant, l'm going out.

466
00:49:13,444 --> 00:49:15,404
-How did you make out?
-Make out?

467
00:49:15,727 --> 00:49:17,138
He darned near kissed me.

468
00:49:17,262 --> 00:49:18,796
-Who?
-The Secretary of War.

469
00:49:19,034 --> 00:49:21,978
We're just licking
the tar out of thorn all along the line.

470
00:49:22,616 --> 00:49:23,792
-Who's next?
-l am.

471
00:49:23,994 --> 00:49:26,747
l hope it's for the President.
l'd like to sec his face

472
00:49:26,907 --> 00:49:28,160
when he gets the good news.

473
00:49:28,325 --> 00:49:31,738
Ready here! Commissary.

474
00:49:33,323 --> 00:49:35,126
For General Scott.
Marked private.

475
00:49:35,409 --> 00:49:36,618
Rush it it's victory!

476
00:49:47,000 --> 00:49:48,518
General Scott, what news?

477
00:49:48,829 --> 00:49:50,877
We're winning, sir. l'm sure of it.

478
00:49:51,534 --> 00:49:54,763
-l wish...
-Come in.

479
00:50:02,546 --> 00:50:05,243
-Rebel resistance broken at Bull Run,-

480
00:50:05,708 --> 00:50:08,981
-our army sweeping everything before it.-

481
00:50:10,966 --> 00:50:14,617
President, if we can
capture Richmond now,

482
00:50:14,852 --> 00:50:16,672
it means the end of the war.

483
00:50:16,834 --> 00:50:19,455
-Ah, thank God.
-And you, sir...

484
00:50:20,034 --> 00:50:23,459
this victory will silence
your enemies forever

485
00:50:23,958 --> 00:50:27,306
and you will be
the greatest president in our history.

486
00:50:27,464 --> 00:50:30,584
lt doesn't matter
what they think of me, General.

487
00:50:31,997 --> 00:50:33,514
-We will have saved the Union.
-Yes.

488
00:50:36,455 --> 00:50:39,878
General, would you mind
letting me have that telegram?

489
00:50:39,961 --> 00:50:41,932
l'd like to show it
to Mrs. Lincoln myself.

490
00:50:42,132 --> 00:50:44,407
Why, certainly, not, Mr. President.

491
00:50:45,332 --> 00:50:48,757
And would you mind
not calling me 'Mr. President'...

492
00:50:49,674 --> 00:50:51,041
just Lincoln.

493
00:50:52,000 --> 00:50:54,091
All right, Lincoln.

494
00:51:01,000 --> 00:51:03,419
Ready here!
Headquarters!

495
00:51:03,809 --> 00:51:05,956
Sergeant, all messages
for General Scott, hold.

496
00:51:06,195 --> 00:51:07,696
All messages for General Scott hold.

497
00:51:08,005 --> 00:51:09,811
All messages for General Scott hold.

498
00:51:10,083 --> 00:51:12,382
Attention! Commanding officer.

499
00:51:16,626 --> 00:51:17,851
At ease!

500
00:51:18,012 --> 00:51:19,161
Ready here!

501
00:51:20,321 --> 00:51:22,544
Bad news?
Give it to me.

502
00:51:29,674 --> 00:51:31,437
Attention the President!

503
00:51:32,213 --> 00:51:33,363
At ease!

504
00:51:33,870 --> 00:51:35,939
Well, General, what news now?

505
00:51:37,872 --> 00:51:39,558
And the first reports?

506
00:51:41,607 --> 00:51:43,368
Over-confidence.

507
00:51:46,301 --> 00:51:48,216
Yes, l should have known.

508
00:51:59,233 --> 00:52:01,345
-impossible to reform lines.-

509
00:52:02,351 --> 00:52:04,267
-The men are a confused mob,-

510
00:52:04,777 --> 00:52:06,505
-entirely demoralized.-

511
00:52:07,086 --> 00:52:10,818
You must make every effort
to save Washington.

512
00:52:26,830 --> 00:52:28,012
- 1 !
- 2!

513
00:52:28,176 --> 00:52:29,403
- 3!
- 4!

514
00:52:29,486 --> 00:52:30,514
- 1 !
- 2!

515
00:52:30,641 --> 00:52:31,789
- 3!
- 4!

516
00:52:31,912 --> 00:52:33,018
- 1 !
- 2!

517
00:52:33,143 --> 00:52:34,292
- 3!
- 4!

518
00:52:34,490 --> 00:52:35,671
- 1 !
- 2!

519
00:52:36,029 --> 00:52:37,331
Battalion!

520
00:52:37,875 --> 00:52:39,178
On right into line, March!

521
00:52:39,376 --> 00:52:40,603
Company, halt!

522
00:52:40,879 --> 00:52:42,106
Left turn!

523
00:52:42,612 --> 00:52:45,150
Right turn!

524
00:52:45,498 --> 00:52:46,646
Halt!

525
00:52:49,000 --> 00:52:51,999
First Company, right by fours.

526
00:53:24,000 --> 00:53:25,962
Well, they're gathering.

527
00:53:33,570 --> 00:53:34,824
The reserves are gathering, sir.

528
00:53:35,106 --> 00:53:37,503
-How many regulars can we muster?
-About five thousand.

529
00:53:38,532 --> 00:53:40,301
-Volunteers?
-Doubtful, sir.

530
00:53:40,698 --> 00:53:42,343
Can we hold Washington?

531
00:53:42,667 --> 00:53:43,921
We'll do our best sir.

532
00:53:50,660 --> 00:53:53,259
Mr. Hay, is it true
we have to get out of Washington?

533
00:53:53,379 --> 00:53:54,553
lt is very serious, madam.

534
00:53:54,795 --> 00:53:57,314
For heaven's sake,
what sort of an army have we anyhow?

535
00:53:57,512 --> 00:53:59,080
They did the best
they could, Mrs. Lincoln.

536
00:53:59,246 --> 00:54:00,576
They might have considered us.

537
00:54:00,702 --> 00:54:02,628
it’ll take forever
to get those trunks packed.

538
00:54:02,788 --> 00:54:05,464
l'm sorry, madam, for all of us.

539
00:54:10,862 --> 00:54:13,572
Mr. Lincoln, l hear we're leaving.

540
00:54:14,327 --> 00:54:16,409
Just a moment, Mary, please.

541
00:54:16,886 --> 00:54:19,238
After all the trouble we had getting here,

542
00:54:19,525 --> 00:54:22,437
l must say we've had a very short stay.

543
00:54:25,550 --> 00:54:27,868
Mary, l've hung up my hat right here,

544
00:54:27,991 --> 00:54:30,902
and here it stays till
they knock it off with a bayonet.

545
00:54:33,426 --> 00:54:37,794
From now on, Mary,
l'm going to run this war.

546
00:54:44,000 --> 00:54:46,169
March! Halt!

547
00:54:47,403 --> 00:54:49,135
Headquarters,
Post Number Four.

548
00:54:50,805 --> 00:54:53,529
Halt! Guard.

549
00:54:53,969 --> 00:54:55,543
Forward March!

550
00:55:04,254 --> 00:55:05,718
Counsel of the accused,

551
00:55:06,154 --> 00:55:08,594
have you nothing further
to say in defense of the prisoner?

552
00:55:09,240 --> 00:55:13,943
The court finds you guilty
and the sentence death.

553
00:55:21,000 --> 00:55:23,362
-Just a minute.
-The President... attention!

554
00:55:30,135 --> 00:55:32,382
You must pardon me,
gentlemen, for this intrusion.

555
00:55:34,681 --> 00:55:37,873
l overheard one of our soldiers
sentenced to death.

556
00:55:38,081 --> 00:55:41,995
Yes, Mr. President.
A bad example of cowardice and desertion.

557
00:55:44,564 --> 00:55:48,277
Well, young man, tell me about it.

558
00:55:50,932 --> 00:55:54,563
l think the findings
of the court were just, sir.

559
00:55:54,000 --> 00:55:55,573
That all you have to say?

560
00:55:57,876 --> 00:56:01,309
Sire, it was our first big battle.

561
00:56:02,267 --> 00:56:04,206
We were trying to take a stone wall.

562
00:56:05,159 --> 00:56:07,204
We'd been trying,
it seems, for years...

563
00:56:07,373 --> 00:56:08,588
Go on.

564
00:56:09,232 --> 00:56:11,765
Finally, though, we got there.

565
00:56:12,912 --> 00:56:15,479
l was fighting bayonets.

566
00:56:16,355 --> 00:56:17,817
We were all crazy...

567
00:56:18,571 --> 00:56:19,910
on top of the wall...

568
00:56:20,785 --> 00:56:21,923
Yes?

569
00:56:22,567 --> 00:56:27,305
There was my boyhood chum,
looking up at me from the ground.

570
00:56:28,819 --> 00:56:34,941
Not alive,
we had killed him a long time ago,

571
00:56:34,942 --> 00:56:36,717
but l knew him.

572
00:56:37,000 --> 00:56:38,183
And then?

573
00:56:39,139 --> 00:56:42,146
l guess l went really crazy, sir.

574
00:56:44,643 --> 00:56:45,780
That's all.

575
00:56:46,028 --> 00:56:48,314
The captain reports
that you threw your rifle away.

576
00:56:48,959 --> 00:56:51,727
Yes, sir. That must be right, sir.

577
00:56:52,323 --> 00:56:54,102
Making excuses, Colton?

578
00:56:54,345 --> 00:56:55,761
No, sir only...

579
00:56:56,639 --> 00:56:58,216
get it over with, quick!

580
00:57:00,000 --> 00:57:06,811
Hanging, killing, blood.

581
00:57:17,883 --> 00:57:19,144
l'm tired.

582
00:57:23,194 --> 00:57:26,948
But my generals are right,
we must maintain discipline.

583
00:57:27,477 --> 00:57:28,616
That's all.

584
00:57:32,155 --> 00:57:33,373
Wait a minute.

585
00:57:35,089 --> 00:57:36,306
Bring that young man back.

586
00:57:40,483 --> 00:57:41,665
l have it!

587
00:57:42,901 --> 00:57:45,752
- it’s a leg case.
-l beg your pardon, sir?

588
00:57:46,506 --> 00:57:48,404
Leg case... yes, yes,

589
00:57:48,530 --> 00:57:52,724
my shelves are full of them,
but not quite like this one.

590
00:57:54,953 --> 00:57:58,472
lf the Lord Almighty
gives a man a cowardly pair of legs,

591
00:57:58,997 --> 00:58:00,339
and that man gets frightened,

592
00:58:01,693 --> 00:58:04,504
he can't help his legs
running away with him, can he?

593
00:58:09,426 --> 00:58:11,320
Young man, l'm going to pardon you.

594
00:58:14,000 --> 00:58:15,818
Go back and do your duty.

595
00:58:17,016 --> 00:58:20,300
Oh, l will, sir! l will!

596
00:58:21,539 --> 00:58:22,883
l'm trusting you.

597
00:58:29,000 --> 00:58:30,264
Well, Senator?

598
00:58:31,974 --> 00:58:35,416
Mr. President, my state of New York

599
00:58:35,665 --> 00:58:38,239
is crying out
against this endless slaughter.

600
00:58:38,442 --> 00:58:39,864
-And mine, sir.

601
00:58:43,201 --> 00:58:47,716
They're holding services here today
for those who were sacrificed,

602
00:58:48,000 --> 00:58:49,501
as they are everywhere.

603
00:58:49,984 --> 00:58:52,116
-From the East to the West, sir.

604
00:58:52,363 --> 00:58:53,677
-lt can't go on, sir.

605
00:58:55,660 --> 00:58:57,998
New England, the West,

606
00:58:58,558 --> 00:59:03,641
the entire country are in mourning
for this useless waste of lives.

607
00:59:05,903 --> 00:59:09,626
Let the Southern States
go there way, Mr. President,

608
00:59:10,229 --> 00:59:11,289
and we will go ours.

609
00:59:11,538 --> 00:59:13,243
But l tell you, Mr. President...

610
00:59:13,562 --> 00:59:17,130
-...out of the wilderness,-
-out of the wilderness.-

611
00:59:17,372 --> 00:59:21,299
-Old Abe Lincoln-
-came out of the wilderness,-

612
00:59:21,539 --> 00:59:24,430
-down in lllinois.-

613
00:59:24,000 --> 00:59:27,459
Daddy, mother won't let me stay up.

614
00:59:27,459 --> 00:59:30,078
She wants me to go to bed,
and l don't want to.

615
00:59:30,276 --> 00:59:33,563
Don't you think it's time
for little boys to be in bed, Tad?

616
00:59:34,012 --> 00:59:37,029
-But l want to stay with you.
-But your mother?

617
00:59:37,193 --> 00:59:40,240
lf you say so, she can't make me.

618
00:59:40,448 --> 00:59:41,842
You're the President.

619
00:59:43,152 --> 00:59:47,955
You're the only person in the country
who thinks l should have any authority.

620
00:59:52,000 --> 00:59:53,867
-Run along now.
-No.

621
00:59:54,147 --> 00:59:58,121
l want to stay with you.
Please, can't l, daddy?

622
00:59:58,484 --> 01:00:02,579
No, we're very busy here now, Tad.
Run along.

623
01:00:03,175 --> 01:00:04,486
All right, daddy.

624
01:00:08,583 --> 01:00:10,246
-Goodnight.
-Goodnight.

625
01:00:11,409 --> 01:00:12,798
Sleep tight.

626
01:00:13,155 --> 01:00:14,302
Goodnight...

627
01:00:21,000 --> 01:00:23,946
Gentlemen,
don't you suppose my heart bleeds

628
01:00:24,146 --> 01:00:27,088
for all the sorrows
this war has brought upon us?

629
01:00:28,324 --> 01:00:32,770
Do you suppose there is a human being
who wants peace more than l do.

630
01:00:34,252 --> 01:00:36,551
But we want lasting peace,

631
01:00:38,192 --> 01:00:42,084
and we can have that only
by preserving the Union.

632
01:00:51,323 --> 01:00:52,827
And, gentlemen...

633
01:00:54,029 --> 01:00:56,769
the Union is going to be preserved!

634
01:02:21,000 --> 01:02:25,334
-And by virtue of the power,-
-and for the purpose aforesaid,-

635
01:02:26,181 --> 01:02:30,101
-l do order and declare-
-that all persons held as slaves -

636
01:02:30,332 --> 01:02:33,834
-within said designated states-
-and parts of states-

637
01:02:34,636 --> 01:02:37,758
-are henceforward-
-and forever shall be...-

638
01:02:38,295 --> 01:02:39,386
-free.-

639
01:02:56,000 --> 01:03:00,454
Well, gentlemen, it is done.

640
01:03:19,000 --> 01:03:20,935
-More bad news, Hay?
-l'm afraid there is.

641
01:03:21,054 --> 01:03:22,146
Well, let's have it.

642
01:03:22,308 --> 01:03:24,396
The Secret Service
reports that there are 600,000

643
01:03:27,101 --> 01:03:28,354
-To get me?
-Yes.

644
01:03:29,461 --> 01:03:31,125
That many ought
to keep me dodging.

645
01:03:31,364 --> 01:03:33,407
We're taking
every precaution to guard you.

646
01:03:34,974 --> 01:03:36,338
Where are they mostly?

647
01:03:37,142 --> 01:03:40,713
Ohio has one 100,000 armed men
ready to rise up and depose you.

648
01:03:40,985 --> 01:03:42,878
Illionois, 135,000.

649
01:03:43,039 --> 01:03:47,561
lllinois,
my old stamping ground...

650
01:03:49,467 --> 01:03:54,289
That makes me feel badly.
You mustn't tell Mary.

651
01:03:54,450 --> 01:03:57,448
But Mr. President,
we are worried about you.

652
01:03:58,443 --> 01:04:02,541
Don't mind me, Hay.
Go on to bed, you're tired.

653
01:04:29,517 --> 01:04:31,756
Lincoln, come on to bed.

654
01:04:33,017 --> 01:04:34,348
l'm worried, Mary.

655
01:04:35,262 --> 01:04:39,059
You can't win this war worrying and
walking around in your stocking feet.

656
01:04:39,862 --> 01:04:41,119
l can't sleep.

657
01:04:41,919 --> 01:04:46,968
Neither can l. At least we could sleep
in Springfield, couldn't we?

658
01:04:48,537 --> 01:04:51,609
Did we ever sleep?

659
01:05:12,159 --> 01:05:14,165
Now, stand still.

660
01:05:19,195 --> 01:05:20,748
l've got it, Mary.

661
01:05:23,451 --> 01:05:26,112
l've found the man to win this war

662
01:05:28,206 --> 01:05:31,702
and his name is Grant.

663
01:05:40,646 --> 01:05:45,544
l'm sorry, Mr. President, but smoking
is one of my most persistent habits.

664
01:05:46,766 --> 01:05:48,963
And winning victories is another.

665
01:05:50,422 --> 01:05:51,587
Thank you.

666
01:05:54,907 --> 01:05:56,724
Then you do believe in me, Sir?

667
01:05:57,683 --> 01:05:59,120
l sent for you.

668
01:06:02,592 --> 01:06:06,658
General, the North is desperate.

669
01:06:07,535 --> 01:06:09,202
We need you.

670
01:06:16,890 --> 01:06:20,419
And, should l assume command,
there will be no interference?

671
01:06:20,544 --> 01:06:22,931
None. l promise you that.

672
01:06:24,955 --> 01:06:26,124
Grant,...

673
01:06:27,046 --> 01:06:29,631
Lieutenant-General is
the highest army command

674
01:06:29,825 --> 01:06:31,491
the President can bestow.

675
01:06:32,866 --> 01:06:35,602
We haven't had one
since General Washington.

676
01:06:37,392 --> 01:06:39,903
He was a fair sort of a soldier too.

677
01:06:41,766 --> 01:06:43,401
Lieutenant-General Grant,...{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ulysses_S._Grant}

678
01:06:44,657 --> 01:06:46,896
my orders are: Win the War.

679
01:06:48,995 --> 01:06:51,687
l'll give my best, Mr. President.

680
01:06:52,454 --> 01:06:53,969
l know you will.

681
01:06:56,106 --> 01:06:59,299
Unfortunately many of us
have failed to recognize

682
01:07:00,331 --> 01:07:02,719
a great Confederate Soldier.

683
01:07:03,411 --> 01:07:04,814
Lee.

684
01:07:06,376 --> 01:07:08,767
On the other hand, we have thus far

685
01:07:09,000 --> 01:07:13,254
failed to take advantage
of a great Northern Soldier.

686
01:07:13,375 --> 01:07:16,186
l hope l am not too late
in correcting the error.

687
01:07:17,670 --> 01:07:22,379
Mr. President,
l think it only fair to warn you

688
01:07:23,378 --> 01:07:25,466
that many people, don't approve of me.

689
01:07:25,735 --> 01:07:27,023
Nor of me.

690
01:07:28,515 --> 01:07:32,989
But rest assured
there will be no interference,

691
01:07:35,245 --> 01:07:36,760
no intrusion.

692
01:07:38,593 --> 01:07:41,437
Well, of all things!
Who locked this door?

693
01:07:42,475 --> 01:07:43,683
My wife!

694
01:07:44,907 --> 01:07:46,236
All right, Mary.

695
01:07:52,094 --> 01:07:54,938
Land sakes!
Where did all the smoke come from?

696
01:07:55,098 --> 01:07:58,940
Mary, meet General Grant. Mrs. Lincoln.

697
01:07:59,586 --> 01:08:03,503
-it’s a pleasure to meet the first lady.
-Thank you.

698
01:08:04,189 --> 01:08:05,901
-l want to talk to you. Mr. Lincoln.
-But Mary, we...

699
01:08:06,056 --> 01:08:09,127
l want to talk to you about discharging
some of the servants in this house.

700
01:08:09,857 --> 01:08:13,157
l don't see how anybody
could talk in here with all this smoke.

701
01:08:14,801 --> 01:08:16,317
Why don't you open the window?

702
01:08:17,730 --> 01:08:18,821
l can't stand it!

703
01:08:19,519 --> 01:08:20,771
Pardon me, General.

704
01:08:21,688 --> 01:08:23,167
l'll have to talk to you later.

705
01:08:26,135 --> 01:08:29,210
-l'm very sorry if...
-There no apologies, General.

706
01:08:30,472 --> 01:08:32,105
You've given me an idea.

707
01:08:33,668 --> 01:08:35,604
l may take up smoking myself.

708
01:08:39,832 --> 01:08:41,463
But to resume, General.

709
01:08:42,871 --> 01:08:44,992
We will give you
all the help you need.

710
01:08:45,877 --> 01:08:49,026
Every man capable
of bearing arms shall go.

711
01:08:50,479 --> 01:08:52,642
We've got to win this war.

712
01:08:54,206 --> 01:08:57,701
lt is a duty we owe
the South as well as the North.

713
01:08:59,455 --> 01:09:03,373
it’s a big job, Mr. President...
a big job.

714
01:09:03,946 --> 01:09:06,180
but thy will be done.

715
01:09:11,628 --> 01:09:13,066
Thy will be done.

716
01:09:19,271 --> 01:09:20,829
We're in a tight place now.

717
01:09:21,286 --> 01:09:23,183
Yes, we generally are, Stanton.

718
01:09:23,418 --> 01:09:25,852
Everything depends upon Sheridan.

719
01:09:26,233 --> 01:09:27,714
He's a fighting Irishman.

720
01:09:27,869 --> 01:09:29,503
Then why doesn't he fight?

721
01:09:36,083 --> 01:09:39,307
-One division Sheridan's-
-army routed.-

722
01:09:39,927 --> 01:09:43,375
-Now trying to hold left flank.-

723
01:09:46,164 --> 01:09:49,852
Oh, the blood it takes
to hold this Union together.

724
01:09:50,232 --> 01:09:55,781
lt will undo everything
Grant has done. it’s hopeless.

725
01:09:56,244 --> 01:09:57,604
l don't think so...

726
01:09:58,753 --> 01:10:02,778
Before each victory l've had
a vision of a ship with white sails.

727
01:10:05,028 --> 01:10:07,041
That vision has just conic to me.

728
01:10:08,262 --> 01:10:11,906
With all respect,
l'd rather trade your ship

729
01:10:12,329 --> 01:10:14,342
for good news from Sheridan.

730
01:10:19,371 --> 01:10:21,228
Is that General Sheridan in there?

731
01:10:22,070 --> 01:10:23,626
Sure 'tis General Sheridan.

732
01:10:36,711 --> 01:10:38,377
Doesn't that sound like cannon fire?

733
01:10:41,313 --> 01:10:43,023
-l don't hear anything, sir.

734
01:11:03,185 --> 01:11:04,547
Hey, come here.

735
01:11:07,180 --> 01:11:08,395
Have you good ears?

736
01:11:08,395 --> 01:11:09,953
Pretty good when they're washed.

737
01:11:11,021 --> 01:11:12,156
Then try them.

738
01:11:13,075 --> 01:11:14,742
Do you hear anything like cannon fire?

739
01:11:20,451 --> 01:11:23,418
it’s cannon all right,
over by the swamp.

740
01:11:23,648 --> 01:11:25,432
They may have caught
General Wright by surprise!

741
01:11:26,310 --> 01:11:27,401
Mount those horses!

742
01:11:38,861 --> 01:11:43,689
-Sheridan's entire army-
-has met an overwhelming defeat.-

743
01:11:46,925 --> 01:11:51,676
incredible.
And no word from Sheridan.

744
01:11:53,163 --> 01:11:54,830
l'm afraid he was away.

745
01:11:57,765 --> 01:12:00,610
lt means the defeat of all our plans.

746
01:12:01,149 --> 01:12:04,948
Yes, for we'll have
to withdraw Grant from Richmond

747
01:12:05,601 --> 01:12:07,386
to protect Washington.

748
01:12:14,386 --> 01:12:16,974
Come on, boys! Come on!

749
01:13:49,093 --> 01:13:51,216
General,
the right wing is in full retreat.

750
01:13:51,492 --> 01:13:52,704
impossible to hold our position, sir.

751
01:13:52,976 --> 01:13:56,427
Tell Colonel Tolliver
to reform and hold at all costs.

752
01:13:56,552 --> 01:13:57,685
We're going back!

753
01:14:03,357 --> 01:14:04,612
Officers, reform your men!

754
01:14:04,880 --> 01:14:06,092
We're going back!

755
01:14:21,047 --> 01:14:24,736
-Come on boys, let's go!
-Let's go, boys!

756
01:14:26,180 --> 01:14:28,223
Forward!
Forward to victory!

757
01:14:45,847 --> 01:14:49,033
-Down with, the traitor,-
-up with the star!-

758
01:14:49,307 --> 01:14:51,043
-While we rally-
-'round the flag boys,-

759
01:14:51,361 --> 01:14:52,841
-We'll rally-
-once again,-

760
01:14:53,223 --> 01:14:56,037
-shouting the battle cry of freedom!-

761
01:14:56,722 --> 01:14:58,356
-Yes, we'll rally 'round the flag, boys-

762
01:14:58,588 --> 01:15:00,175
-We'll rally once again,-

763
01:15:00,567 --> 01:15:03,866
-Shouting the battle cry of freedom,-

764
01:15:04,331 --> 01:15:07,748
-We will rally from the hillside,-
-We'll gather from the plain.-

765
01:15:08,210 --> 01:15:11,584
-Shouting the battle cry of freedom,-

766
01:15:12,125 --> 01:15:15,890
-The Union forever,-
-hurrah, boys, hurrah!-

767
01:15:16,081 --> 01:15:19,348
-Sown with the traitor,-
-up with the star.-

768
01:15:19,468 --> 01:15:21,554
-While we rally round the flag boys,-

769
01:15:21,789 --> 01:15:23,529
-We'll rally once again,-

770
01:15:23,803 --> 01:15:27,074
-Shouting the battle cry of freedom,-

771
01:15:39,361 --> 01:15:42,963
-Arrived in time to reform forces.-

772
01:15:43,471 --> 01:15:44,950
-Have struck the enemy-

773
01:15:45,676 --> 01:15:48,971
-and have won an
overwhelming victory. Sheridan.-

774
01:15:50,276 --> 01:15:51,446
l knew it.

775
01:15:52,559 --> 01:15:53,726
l knew it...

776
01:15:59,022 --> 01:16:04,682
Better and better. Look! Here
Tremendous number of prisoners.

777
01:16:06,403 --> 01:16:07,735
Prisoners?

778
01:16:09,368 --> 01:16:13,440
l hope there's nothing
but prisoners from now on.

779
01:16:17,926 --> 01:16:21,120
The ship, Stanton!

780
01:16:22,458 --> 01:16:23,969
Yes, Mr. President,

781
01:16:24,927 --> 01:16:26,179
and Sheridan!

782
01:17:29,514 --> 01:17:30,872
Surrender...

783
01:17:32,213 --> 01:17:34,104
my poor army!

784
01:17:35,484 --> 01:17:38,480
l'd rather die a thousand deaths
than do that to them.

785
01:17:38,905 --> 01:17:42,281
There, General,
you must lie down and rest.

786
01:17:44,762 --> 01:17:45,973
Rest...

787
01:17:47,921 --> 01:17:49,704
that's a beautiful word...

788
01:18:29,304 --> 01:18:32,992
They've caught a spy and they want
the order for his execution approved.

789
01:18:51,705 --> 01:18:52,799
Colonel Marshall...

790
01:18:54,182 --> 01:18:55,314
who was that?

791
01:18:55,510 --> 01:18:58,019
-Only a courier, sir.
-What did he want?

792
01:18:58,135 --> 01:19:01,055
They've caught a spy.
l approved the execution order, sir.

793
01:19:01,558 --> 01:19:02,690
Colonel,...

794
01:19:03,727 --> 01:19:08,326
the only reason for shooting a spy
is the protection of an army, isn't it?

795
01:19:08,708 --> 01:19:09,876
Yes, sir.

796
01:19:10,839 --> 01:19:12,244
Well,...

797
01:19:13,424 --> 01:19:16,615
you and l know
that this army can't exist much longer.

798
01:19:18,749 --> 01:19:19,883
isn’t that right?

799
01:19:21,298 --> 01:19:22,511
Yes, sir.

800
01:19:23,123 --> 01:19:26,692
That is why l am unwilling
that there shall be a single life lost

801
01:19:27,307 --> 01:19:28,671
unnecessarily.

802
01:19:32,936 --> 01:19:34,105
Colonel...

803
01:19:34,458 --> 01:19:38,222
l wonder if you'd mind
countermanding that order.

804
01:19:40,163 --> 01:19:41,373
l will.
Yes, sir.

805
01:20:20,746 --> 01:20:22,077
Tired, Grant?

806
01:20:23,635 --> 01:20:25,045
Not much.

807
01:20:44,060 --> 01:20:45,846
Mr. President, we have them.

808
01:20:46,724 --> 01:20:49,344
lt can only last a few days more.

809
01:20:50,643 --> 01:20:53,639
General Sherman and l are glad
of this chance to talk with you.

810
01:20:54,717 --> 01:20:58,507
The Union...
we've saved it at last.

811
01:20:58,975 --> 01:21:00,638
They must surrender soon.

812
01:21:01,750 --> 01:21:02,914
The Union...

813
01:21:04,145 --> 01:21:05,855
We'll have 'em all back...

814
01:21:06,921 --> 01:21:10,342
United, free...
one Country.

815
01:21:11,030 --> 01:21:12,665
And meanwhile, Mr. President?

816
01:21:13,770 --> 01:21:17,684
l've heard the country
wishes all rebel property confiscated

817
01:21:18,636 --> 01:21:23,310
and the rebel generals,
such as Lee, shot for treason.

818
01:21:24,418 --> 01:21:26,462
He's put up a grand battle.

819
01:21:28,563 --> 01:21:31,529
And they've robbed
the cradle and the grave, sir.

820
01:21:32,443 --> 01:21:35,408
Lee is fighting with his last breath.

821
01:21:37,616 --> 01:21:39,132
Shoot Robert E. Lee?{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Robert_E._Lee}

822
01:21:40,128 --> 01:21:42,941
Someone will have to shoot
Abraham Lincoln first.

823
01:21:44,729 --> 01:21:46,893
They're rebels, not traitors.

824
01:21:48,228 --> 01:21:50,574
And their horses and baggage, sir?

825
01:21:51,995 --> 01:21:54,157
They'll need them
for the spring plowing.

826
01:21:55,266 --> 01:21:56,933
Let 'em keep 'em and get to work.

827
01:21:58,117 --> 01:21:59,448
Very good, sir.

828
01:22:00,248 --> 01:22:02,714
Just one thing more, Mr. President...

829
01:22:03,516 --> 01:22:07,086
the head of the rebel government
Jefferson Davis?{https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Jefferson_Davis}

830
01:22:09,831 --> 01:22:11,043
Jefferson Davis...

831
01:22:11,544 --> 01:22:13,214
Do you wish his capture?

832
01:22:16,186 --> 01:22:18,271
That reminds me of a story.

833
01:22:19,342 --> 01:22:21,127
We had a terrible drunkard once

834
01:22:23,148 --> 01:22:24,358
in Springfield.

835
01:22:25,770 --> 01:22:27,814
Finally he signed the pledge.

836
01:22:27,901 --> 01:22:29,380
Next day he got thirsty

837
01:22:29,532 --> 01:22:31,731
and went to a bar
and ordered a lemonade.

838
01:22:32,730 --> 01:22:37,024
While the bartender was fixing it,
the old drunk got sadder and sadder.

839
01:22:38,280 --> 01:22:40,524
Finally he leaned over
and said to the bartender,

840
01:22:42,049 --> 01:22:45,123
''Mike, while you're fixin' that,

841
01:22:45,396 --> 01:22:47,287
couldn't you put
a nice little shot of whiskey in,

842
01:22:47,524 --> 01:22:49,083
unbeknownst to myself?''

843
01:22:49,692 --> 01:22:50,947
Well, sir?

844
01:22:52,000 --> 01:22:54,202
Couldn't you sort
of lot Jeff Davis escape,

845
01:22:55,542 --> 01:22:57,214
unbeknownst to yourselves?

846
01:22:58,744 --> 01:23:00,112
We'll do our part, sir.

847
01:23:04,078 --> 01:23:07,685
We're going to take them back
as though they'd never been away.

848
01:23:15,738 --> 01:23:17,103
Lincoln...

849
01:23:21,190 --> 01:23:22,856
He freed the negroes.

850
01:23:25,723 --> 01:23:28,571
He's suppressed
the right of a trail by jury.

851
01:23:31,933 --> 01:23:33,297
He muzzled the press!

852
01:23:38,334 --> 01:23:40,532
Now, with the aid of this ''butternuts'',

853
01:23:41,341 --> 01:23:43,434
his army and his negroes,
he make himself

854
01:23:43,516 --> 01:23:44,925
King of the America.

855
01:23:47,248 --> 01:23:48,385
Lincoln!

856
01:23:53,419 --> 01:23:54,754
l drink to him...

857
01:23:57,881 --> 01:24:00,469
and his domation!

858
01:24:05,727 --> 01:24:07,018
Right,...

859
01:24:07,292 --> 01:24:08,620
you're right.

860
01:24:09,233 --> 01:24:10,871
On my part...

861
01:24:15,978 --> 01:24:18,874
Go on,
you're among friends...

862
01:24:21,655 --> 01:24:25,610
The man who kills Abraham Lincoln

863
01:24:26,757 --> 01:24:28,505
will be immortal.

864
01:24:31,182 --> 01:24:32,469
Now, listen...

865
01:24:38,305 --> 01:24:39,750
l have a plan...

866
01:24:41,314 --> 01:24:42,450
and l'm going in.

867
01:24:54,615 --> 01:24:57,084
Do you mind my smoking, Mrs. Lincoln?

868
01:24:57,391 --> 01:24:59,411
No, not much.

869
01:25:01,510 --> 01:25:04,783
You don't know how glad we all are
that you were reelected, Mr. President.

870
01:25:05,357 --> 01:25:06,690
l'm sure this lady will be glad

871
01:25:06,919 --> 01:25:08,360
to spend four more years
in the White House.

872
01:25:08,480 --> 01:25:10,759
Glad? l've just hired two new maids.

873
01:25:13,132 --> 01:25:16,361
Lincoln, will you ever learn
to keep your feet in shoes?

874
01:25:18,465 --> 01:25:21,740
Mrs. Lincoln,
have you met General Grant?

875
01:25:21,971 --> 01:25:25,093
l met him. lt took us a week
to get the smoke out of the curtains.

876
01:25:28,030 --> 01:25:31,408
Yes, Oglesby, he's one
human being who has faults.

877
01:25:32,407 --> 01:25:34,851
A party of prohibitionists,
called on me the other day

878
01:25:35,118 --> 01:25:36,749
and complained about Grant's drinking.

879
01:25:37,745 --> 01:25:40,257
l told 'em if l knew what brand he uses,

880
01:25:40,523 --> 01:25:42,617
l'd send barrels of it
to my other generals.

881
01:25:44,527 --> 01:25:46,349
Two new maids...

882
01:25:47,650 --> 01:25:50,122
lf we're going to be
here four more years...

883
01:25:52,149 --> 01:25:57,099
Four years...
four more years...

884
01:25:57,441 --> 01:26:00,414
Mr. Lincoln, don't you go thinking
about of those dreams again.

885
01:26:00,948 --> 01:26:04,828
You'll live two hundred and after
we'll celebrate it right over here,

886
01:26:05,559 --> 01:26:09,361
we'll travel around the world
and have a nice long rest.

887
01:26:10,475 --> 01:26:13,931
Oh, Daddy!,
Would that be fun?

888
01:26:14,210 --> 01:26:17,631
-You're not going without me...?
-Why, of course not, Tad.

889
01:26:21,297 --> 01:26:25,444
You can depend on Mother Surratt,
and everything is straight with me.

890
01:26:26,327 --> 01:26:28,914
l said l'd go through with it,
and l will.

891
01:26:29,751 --> 01:26:33,104
Tonight will be remembered
throughout the ages.

892
01:26:35,392 --> 01:26:38,165
l play my best part.

893
01:26:43,740 --> 01:26:47,083
How much better a dagger would look,

894
01:26:50,558 --> 01:26:52,573
Cassius used a dagger,

895
01:26:56,461 --> 01:26:58,100
but this is safer.

896
01:27:23,821 --> 01:27:26,638
-Speech!
-Speech!

897
01:27:31,938 --> 01:27:37,723
-Speech!
-Speech!

898
01:27:46,872 --> 01:27:50,024
Again l say,

899
01:27:51,750 --> 01:27:53,993
with malice toward none,

900
01:27:55,331 --> 01:27:57,497
with charity for all,

901
01:27:59,751 --> 01:28:01,461
with firmness in the right

902
01:28:01,654 --> 01:28:04,052
as God gives us to see the right,

903
01:28:05,164 --> 01:28:09,343
we shall bind up
the nation's wounds and cherish peace.

904
01:28:13,695 --> 01:28:20,890
That government of the people,
by the people, for the people

905
01:28:21,925 --> 01:28:24,821
shall not perish from the earth.

906
01:28:29,130 --> 01:28:30,224
Thank you.

907
01:28:34,195 --> 01:28:35,528
God bless you all.

908
01:28:49,244 --> 01:28:53,472
Mr. Lincoln, l'm just proud of you!

909
01:28:57,055 --> 01:28:59,800
-Peanuts, hold the horse for me?
-Yes.

910
01:29:04,714 --> 01:29:06,079
Will you be long, sir?

911
01:29:08,601 --> 01:29:10,118
No, not very long.

912
01:29:14,888 --> 01:29:17,813
Dundreary,
would you mind bringing me a wrap?

913
01:29:18,200 --> 01:29:19,610
l feel a draught.

914
01:29:19,957 --> 01:29:22,547
l'm afraid you must be mistaken.

915
01:29:23,195 --> 01:29:24,605
Mr. Lincoln...

916
01:29:25,099 --> 01:29:27,115
has just stopped the draft.

917
01:29:51,275 --> 01:29:53,941
l reckon l'm going
to feel right to home here, b'gosh!

918
01:29:55,046 --> 01:29:57,863
l would be too if it wasn't for
that 'ternal servant-critter

919
01:29:58,095 --> 01:30:00,341
with brass buttons on his coat.

920
01:30:04,347 --> 01:30:08,680
He just swells out his big bosom
like a turkey cock in layin' time,

921
01:30:10,174 --> 01:30:13,748
looks at me as if l meant
to esquatulate with the spoons.

922
01:30:16,498 --> 01:30:20,422
l reckon they don't know who they're
dealin' with, but l'll show 'em.

923
01:30:21,148 --> 01:30:24,116
l'm as obstinate
as Deacon Stumpp's forelock.

924
01:30:25,262 --> 01:30:27,353
''lt wouldn't lie down; it couldn't stand up,''

925
01:30:27,814 --> 01:30:30,710
wouldn't point forward and couldn't go backward.

926
01:30:32,653 --> 01:30:35,398
l don't know what
they're going to do with me,

927
01:30:36,008 --> 01:30:39,432
but whatever they do do
and wherever they put me,

928
01:30:39,704 --> 01:30:41,871
l hope it's outa
the reach of that jackass.

929
01:30:43,134 --> 01:30:45,905
l'm a real hoss, l am,

930
01:30:45,990 --> 01:30:48,460
and l get kinda riley with them critters.

931
01:30:49,916 --> 01:30:53,871
They say l don't know
the manners of good society too,

932
01:30:55,403 --> 01:30:58,784
but l guess l know enough
to turn that old gal inside out,

933
01:30:59,936 --> 01:31:02,060
the sock-dollaging old mantrap.

934
01:31:02,640 --> 01:31:05,383
But l dare no more kill her

935
01:31:05,652 --> 01:31:07,437
than l dare ask Queen Victoria

936
01:31:07,785 --> 01:31:10,106
to dance a Cape Cod reel.

937
01:31:19,939 --> 01:31:23,137
Sic semper tyrannis!

938
01:31:25,920 --> 01:31:28,665
Mr. Lincoln,
Mr. Lincoln has been shot!

939
01:31:42,000 --> 01:31:44,743
Get a doctor!
Somebody get a doctor!

